---
title: Custom controls
description: Replace the elements Formsnap renders with `child` snippets, or build a fully custom widget with the headless hooks.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

A plain `<input {...props} />` covers most fields. When the widget is your own element — a star rating, a styled error list, a small instead of a paragraph — Formsnap gives you two escape hatches: `child` snippets that replace the element a component renders, and the `useFormControl`/`useFormField` hooks for widgets that live in their own component.

## Replace elements with `child` snippets

`Label`, `Description` and `FieldErrors` all accept a `child` snippet. The snippet receives `props`; spreading them onto your element is what carries the ID, the label's `for`, and the registration of the mounted node. `bind:ref` hands the actual node back to you.

```svelte title="src/routes/profile/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Description, Field, FieldErrors, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";

	let { data } = $props();

	const form = superForm(
		untrack(() => data.form),
		{ resetForm: false }
	);
	const { form: formData, enhance } = form;

	let nicknameLabel = $state<HTMLElement | null>(null);
	let nicknameDescription = $state<HTMLElement | null>(null);
</script>

{#snippet customLabel({ props }: { props: Record<string, unknown> })}
	<label {...props} data-custom="label">Nickname</label>
{/snippet}

{#snippet customDescription({ props }: { props: Record<string, unknown> })}
	<small {...props} data-custom="description">Two characters minimum.</small>
{/snippet}

{#snippet customErrors({
	props,
	errors,
	errorProps,
}: {
	props: Record<string, unknown>;
	errors: string[];
	errorProps: Record<string, unknown>;
})}
	<ul {...props} data-custom="errors">
		{#each errors as error, index (index)}
			<li {...errorProps}>{error}</li>
		{/each}
	</ul>
{/snippet}

<form method="POST" use:enhance>
	<Field {form} name="nickname">
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label child={customLabel} bind:ref={nicknameLabel} />
				<input {...props} bind:value={$formData.nickname} />
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		<Description child={customDescription} bind:ref={nicknameDescription} />
		<FieldErrors child={customErrors} />
	</Field>

	<button type="submit">Save profile</button>
</form>
```

A snippet declared at the top level and passed as a prop needs an explicit parameter type, because it is not an inline child of the component.

## Build a widget with the hooks

When the control is its own component, it cannot receive snippet `props` as an argument. `useFormControl` reads the enclosing `Control`'s context and returns the same `id`, `name` and ARIA props:

```svelte title="src/routes/profile/rating-control.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { useFormControl } from "@emmorts/formsnap";

	let {
		value = $bindable(4),
		ref = $bindable<HTMLInputElement | null>(null),
	}: { value?: number; ref?: HTMLInputElement | null } = $props();

	const control = useFormControl({});
</script>

<input type="range" min="1" max="5" step="1" {...control.props} bind:value bind:this={ref} />
```

Render it under a `Control` like any other input:

```svelte title="src/routes/profile/+page.svelte"
<Field {form} name="rating" fieldErrors>
	<Control>
		<Label>Rating</Label>
		<RatingControl bind:value={$formData.rating} bind:ref={ratingControl} />
	</Control>
	{#snippet description()}One to five stars.{/snippet}
</Field>
```

A range input submits a string, so the schema coerces it back to a number:

```ts title="src/routes/profile/schema.ts"
import { z } from "zod/v3";

export const profileSchema = z.object({
	nickname: z.string().min(2, "Use at least two characters."),
	rating: z.coerce
		.number()
		.int("Pick a whole number of stars.")
		.min(1, "Pick at least one star.")
		.max(5, "Five stars is the maximum.")
		.default(4),
});

export type ProfileData = z.infer<typeof profileSchema>;
```

## How it works

- `child` replaces the element, not the behaviour. A snippet that renders an element but ignores the supplied `props` produces no association.
- `useFormControl` returns `id`, `labelProps` and `props`. Spreading `props` matches what a Control's `children` snippet would give you.
- `useFormField` works one level up and exposes the field's `form`, `name`, `errors`, `constraints`, `tainted`, `errorsId` and `descriptionId`. Both hooks must be called during component initialization, inside a component rendered under their provider.
- Multiple `Label` components under one `Control` keep their own IDs and refs, and all target that control. `useFormControl({}).labelProps.id` selects the most recently registered active label for headless naming; removing it restores a surviving label, or the default headless ID when none remain.

<Callout type="warning">

Context is required: `useFormControl` needs an enclosing `Control`, and `useFormField` needs an enclosing `Field`, `Fieldset` or `ElementField`. A `Field` alone does not provide `Control` context — a missing provider throws an error naming the required parent. When you rely on the default headless ID for naming, you must render an element with that ID yourself.

</Callout>

The [headless-hook reference](/docs/reference/headless-hooks) documents returned getters, structured message types, provider lifetime and caller-owned association targets.
